    "created_at": "2016-10-13T05:47:24Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-10-13T05:47:24Z",
    "body": "> \"Editable element\" is probably not great since it collides with https://w3c.github.io/editing/execCommand.html#editable.\n\nIndeed, I was not aware of that conflict. The term Editable Element was meant as a local shortcut for the purpose of the CSS spec that needed this term, rather than as a globally usable definition, but conflicts are still bad.\n\n> The closest I can find is \"elements for which the select() method applies.\" See the \"applies\" table to get an idea why.\n\nThis does seems to give us the correct subset of types from the input element. On the other hand, I may be reading the spec wrong, but the definition of `apply` seems to be restricted to the input element, so I'd still need to list the textarea element explicitely. Do I also need to somehow worry about hypothetical future form elements that are neither a type of `input` nor `textarea`s? This definition also doesn't cover the contenteditable elements or designmode...\n\nI can list all these things exhaustively in my definition, but having one entry point in the HTML spec that worries about being (and staying) exhaustive that I can just point to would be nice.\n\nAt the same time, since CSS does apply to other languages than HTML, I'll probably need to stick with a high level generic definition in the CSS spec after all, and merely point to what we're discussing here as an instance of that \"In the case of HTML, this corresponds to....\".\n",
